WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.472 --> 00:00:10.930
KATHRYN PARKER BOUDETT: One thing we've learned in our work

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:15.070
is that human beings have a tendency to see patterns, make inferences

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.700
and judgments, and draw conclusions quite

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.710
quickly from just a little bit of data.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.020
This is an important skill.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.410
It allows us to make thoughtful inferences and predictions.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.590
But it can also lead to bad or misinformed judgments

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:33.280
when we are too quick to draw inferences from a small amount of data.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.700
In our work with schools, we have found that a mental model called

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.330
the Ladder of Inference is especially useful in helping

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:46.290
educators resist this tendency and stay focused on the evidence.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.880
The Ladder of Inference was first developed by business theorists Chris

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.490
Argyris and popularized by Peter Senge and colleagues in a book

00:00:54.490 --> 00:01:00.590
called The Fifth Discipline, the art and practice of the learning organization.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.550
The Ladder of Inference is a metaphor, organized

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.260
around how we as humans tend to make patterns

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.810
about what we see in the world.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:14.480
Whenever I perceive something, I first select some data to focus on.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.800
Then, I add some interpretation, draw conclusions, and finally take actions.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.610
The actions I take then influence what kind of data I collect next.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.760
And for that reason, it can be very dangerous to go up

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.710
the ladder too quickly.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.660
At Data Wise, we like to use this model to help each other maintain

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.190
a relentless focus on evidence.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:40.290
When we hear somebody add interpretations or draw conclusions

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:45.190
too quickly, we try to use phrases like, that statement is pretty high up

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:46.050
on the ladder.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.950
What evidence are you using?

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.350
Or, we're all making a lot of interpretations here.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:55.740
Let's try to go back down that ladder and talk first about the evidence.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.330
CANDICE: That might be a good instructional suggestion.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.620
But let's stick with, maybe, what we wonder

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.120
about what the student was thinking.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.120
MOLLY: I jumped up on the ladder.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.700
KATHRYN PARKER BOUDETT: For example, imagine an administrator

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.250
pops into a classroom and notices that there's

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.980
lots of students chatting at once, but the teacher is over in the corner

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.710
paying attention to just one student.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:18.430
If the administrator adds the interpretation

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:21.360
that the student is distracting the teacher from his work

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.680
and that the other children are taking advantage of the situation

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.510
and just fooling around, the administrator

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.350
might draw the conclusion that the teacher has classroom management

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:35.190
problems and, then, put a negative note in the teacher's file.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:39.200
In that example, the administrator jumped from her observation

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.770
that multiple students were talking once and that the teacher was only

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:46.940
talking to one student-- both of which were true facts.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.480
But she interpreted that the teacher was distracted

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.900
and that the students were off task, not taking the time

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.870
to investigate whether other interpretations could

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.730
have been equally possible.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:03.090
For example, maybe the teacher was deliberately giving one-on-one feedback

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.060
to each student that day and the rest of the students

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.560
were supposed to be engaged in small group discussions.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:13.780
By using the Ladder of Inference to help you note when you're making inferences,

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:18.090
you can catch yourself every time you treat an interpretation

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.160
as though it were a fact.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.920
Because this discipline can be so helpful,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.340
this course will give you lots of practice in starting low on the ladder

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:32.170
and making a conscious choice whenever you want to climb up it.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.220
For each step of the Data Wise course, you'll

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.030
have a chance to look at an artifact from a school

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:40.236
and make observations that are grounded in the data.

00:03:40.236 --> 00:03:44.420
A very helpful way of sticking to evidence that Data Wise schools use

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:48.390
is a protocol called, I notice, I wonder.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:52.230
First, we focus on statements about what we notice.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:56.947
These statements should be as specific and nonjudgmental as possible.

00:03:56.947 --> 00:04:01.090
Then, we wonder about what we noticed.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.720
These wonderings start to climb up the ladder

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.570
to include interpretations of the data.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.750
But they're framed as questions since we are less certain of them.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:15.010
Wonderings allow us to ask meaningful questions about the evidence,

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.920
while also providing a space to let us acknowledge the inferences

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.519
that our minds may be jumping to.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:24.520
You will then have a chance to see how members of the Data Wise team

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.290
collaboratively notice and wonder when making

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:30.500
sense of a wide range of data sources.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.300
This exercise will help you cultivate the E of the ACE habits of mind,

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:37.910
a relentless focus on evidence.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

